ublic health Doc's  some people's are using e-cigarettes to  help them get off regular cigarettes  what does the research say about whether  or not that's a good idea I think the  research says very simply the jury's  still out these cigarettes are a  delivery vehicle for nicotine is  nicotine goes on a carrier molecule  which gets it into the lung the effect  of that carrier  in the lung really isn't known yet and  so alternatives I like the concept but  we still need the data to show that  e-cigarettes are safe okay our next  topic deals with the financial burden of  cancer it was an issue address this week  in a new report by the president's  cancer panel the focus was on the price  of cancer drugs especially recently  developed medications in part of the  panel's public letter to the president  they wrote the challenge at hand is  complex innovative drugs offer new hope  for patients to achieve long term  remissions even cures but virtually all  new cancer drugs enter the market with a  price tag that exceeds a 100 thousand  dollars per year what were some of the  key recommendations here dr. agus well  this is a problem right every new cancer  drug is a hundred to two hundred  thousand dollars and so they have a  monopoly in a sense they're the only one  to treat a certain stage of a certain  disease and so it's predatory practice  and so this committee said listen we  have to stop this it's not sustainable  let's figure out a way to praise the  price drugs based on value so the drugs  that have few side effects and work  really well probably deserve a higher  price tag whereas the ones that cause  lots of side effects or don't work as  well don't and let's figure out a value  system for the patient and the system  where we can have a logical pricing of  drugs that's really interesting the  report showed some also showed some  interesting data when it comes to the  launch of new cancer drugs in comparison  to income what exactly did it show I  that was a somewhat depressing figure in  the paper where they had income totally  flat and they had the price of cancer  drugs at launch go up like this and so  when incomes not changing and every year  drugs are getting more expensive you  know it's not going to have to be  sustainable going forward it's not right  job there is also news about over  payment for prescription drugs what can  you tell us about that that's the  wildest study coming out of University  of Southern California what they showed  is is that let's say you go and you go  to get a medicine for your asthma and  they say their copay is twenty dollars  well many times in fact in the study 23%  of the time the copay was more than the  drug costs on a generic basis  and the pharmacies in the country have a  gag order from the pharmaceutical  Bennett Matt a benefit manager then they  can't tell you that listen if you say  I'm gonna pay cash it'll be seven bucks  but paying with insurance it's 20 bucks  because you have that copay so something  is wrong in that system that gag order  makes no sense yeah it certainly makes  no sense sounds like some reforms are  needed dr. David agus thank you as  always for rising early and giving us  the good word on health and medicine
 

